artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a supersymmetric extension of the Standard Model in which supersymmetry is spontaneously broken in a hidden sector that has no renormalizable couplings to the visible (Standard Model) sector. The visible sector must nonetheless acquire soft supersymmetry-breaking parameters (gaugino masses, scalar masses, trilinear A-terms, and B-terms) that (i) lift the tree-level supertrace sum rule Str M^2 = 0 within the visible sector, and (ii) do not introduce flavor-changing neutral currents or CP-violating phases beyond observed bounds.

Identify the mechanism by which information about hidden-sector supersymmetry breaking is communicated to the visible sector. Candidate mechanisms include:
(a) Gravity (supergravity) mediation, in which the communication proceeds through non-renormalizable operators suppressed by powers of the Planck scale M_Pl, generating soft masses of order m_soft ~ F/M_Pl where sqrt(F) is the SUSY-breaking scale;
(b) Gauge mediation, in which messenger superfields charged under the Standard Model gauge group couple to the SUSY-breaking sector and induce visible-sector soft masses through Standard Model gauge loops, with m_soft ~ (alpha/4 pi) F/M_mess;
(c) Anomalous (or pseudo-anomalous) U(1) mediation through a Fayet–Iliopoulos D-term of an extra U(1) gauge symmetry whose anomaly is canceled by a Green–Schwarz mechanism;
(d) Other mechanisms (anomaly mediation, gaugino mediation, moduli mediation, etc.).

Determine which mediation mechanism is realized in nature, and specify experimental observables (e.g., the identity, mass, and lifetime of the lightest supersymmetric particle; whether the gravitino is the LSP and whether NLSP decays into it have observable displaced vertices; sparticle mass relations and flavor structure; ratios of gaugino masses; the pattern of slepton and squark mass splittings) that allow one to discriminate among the above mechanisms.
